'''{{lang|de|Ein feste Burg ist unser Gott}}''' (''A Mighty Fortress Is Our God''), [[BWV]]&nbsp;80 (also: BWV&nbsp;80.3),{{sfn|Bach Digital 1|2016}} is a [[chorale cantata]] for [[Reformation Day]] by [[Johann Sebastian Bach]]. He reworked it from one of [[Weimar cantata (Bach)|his Weimar cantatas]], [[Alles, was von Gott geboren, BWV 80a|''Alles, was von Gott geboren'', BWV 80a]] (also: BWV&nbsp;80.1).{{sfn|Bach Digital 2|2016}} The first [[Leipzig]] version of the [[church cantata]], BWV&nbsp;80b (also: BWV&nbsp;80.2),{{sfn|Bach Digital 3|2016}} may have been composed as early as 1723, some five months after Bach had moved to Leipzig. Some years later he reworked the cantata one more time, writing an extended [[chorale fantasia]] as its opening [[Movement (music)|movement]]. The text of the BWV&nbsp;80a version was written by [[Salomon Franck]] and contained one stanza of [[Martin Luther]]'s [[hymn]] "[[Ein feste Burg ist unser Gott]]"; for [[Chorale cantata (Bach)|his chorale cantata]] versions, BWV&nbsp;80b and 80, Bach added the complete text of this [[Lutheran hymn]].

Bach scored the cantata for four vocal soloists, a [[SATB|four-part choir]] and a [[Baroque instruments|Baroque chamber ensemble]] of up to three [[oboe]]s of different kinds, strings and continuo. After his death, his son [[Wilhelm Friedemann Bach]] arranged the first and fifth movements, adopting a new text and adding trumpets and timpani.

{{lang|de|Ein feste Burg ist unser Gott}} was published in 1821, the first of Bach's cantatas published after his death. The [[Bach Gesellschaft]] edition appeared half a century later, and included an extended instrumentation by [[Wilhelm Friedemann Bach|Wilhelm Friedemann]].

== History, hymn and compositions ==
Bach wrote the cantata in Leipzig for [[List of church cantatas by liturgical occasion#Reformation Day (31 October)|Reformation Day]], celebrated annually on 31 October.{{sfn|Dürr|2006|p=707}} In composing this work, Bach reused an earlier cantata, [[Alles, was von Gott geboren, BWV 80a|''Alles, was von Gott geboren'', BWV 80a]], which he wrote in [[Weimar]] for the third Sunday in [[Lent]] and based on a text published in 1715 by [[Salomon Franck]].{{sfn|Bach Digital 2|2016}}{{sfn|Dürr|2006|p=709}} This work, on which the Leipzig versions were based, was one of a few in which Bach quoted the same hymn twice, in a closing chorale and in an instrumental [[cantus firmus]] in the opening movement.{{sfn|Dürr|2006|p=20}} In BWV 80a, he used the second stanza of Luther's hymn "Ein feste Burg ist unser Gott" ("[[A Mighty Fortress Is Our God]]"), which is "Mit unser Macht ist nichts getan" (With our might nothing is done),{{sfn|Dürr|2006|p=257}} for the closing chorale.{{sfn|Dürr|2006|p=255}} Bach could not use the earlier work at its intended time in Leipzig, because cantata music was prohibited there during Lent.{{sfn|Dürr|2006|p=709}}

The hymn "Ein feste Burg ist unser Gott" was written by Luther in the late 1520s and became a symbol of the [[Reformation]], adapted in compositions through the centuries, such as in Mendelssohn's ''[[Reformation Symphony]]'', Meyerbeer's opera ''[[Les Huguenots]]'',<ref>{{cite web |title=Tracing Martin Luther's great hymn through musical history |publisher= [[Gramophone (magazine)|Gramophone]] |date=November 29, 2017 |language=en |url= https://www.gramophone.co.uk/features/article/playlist-influential-ein-feste-burg }}</ref> and Debussy's ''[[En blanc et noir]]''.<ref name="Laki">{{cite web | last = Laki | first = Peter | url = https://www.kennedy-center.org/artist/composition/5401 | title = En Blanc et Noir / About the Work | website = [[Kennedy Center]] | access-date = 7 November 2019 }}</ref>

The early Leipzig version (BWV 80b) began with a simple chorale setting of the hymn as the opening [[Movement (music)|movement]].{{sfn|Wolff|1991|p=155}}{{sfn|Bach Digital 3|2016}} This is the only version of the cantata for which autograph pages have survived. The surviving fragments are the only evidence of the BWV 80b version and give a very incomplete picture of it; they ended up in three libraries in two continents &ndash; the [[Polish Library in Paris|Bibliothèque Polonaise de Paris]], the [[National Library of Russia|Rossijskaja nacional'naja biblioteka]] in [[Saint Petersburg]], and the [[Princeton University]] Library in New Jersey.<ref name="BWV80b">Work {{BDW|0101}} (17/6/2017), and subsequent manuscript pages [https://www.bach-digital.de/receive/BachDigitalSource_source_00003630 F-Ppo A. Mickiewicz Rkp. 973] (23/6/2017), [https://www.bach-digital.de/receive/BachDigitalSource_source_00004042 RUS-SPsc BWV 80b] (26/7/2017) and [https://www.bach-digital.de/receive/BachDigitalSource_source_00004338 US-PRscheide BWV 80b] (19/7/2017), at [[Bach Digital]] website</ref> It is not certain when Bach wrote this version of the cantata. Two fragments are on paper with 1723 watermarks, leading musicologists like [[Christoph Wolff]] to conclude that Bach may have written a version of ''Ein feste Burg ist unser Gott'' as part of his [[Bach's first cantata cycle|first cantata cycle]].{{sfn|Wolff|1991|p=155}} [[Alfred Dürr]] proposes a time frame of 1728 to 1731 for its initial composition.{{sfn|Dürr|2006|p=709}}

The 80b version may have already contained all four stanzas of Luther's hymn.{{sfn|Wolff|1991|p=157}} The first stanza appears in the new first movement, the second is a cantus firmus in the second movement (the first movement of the Weimar cantata), and the other two may have been used in the fifth and eighth movements; both of these were chorale settings, as in the later Leipzig version (80.3).{{sfn|Wolff|1991|p=157}}{{sfn|Dürr|2006|pp=709–710}} Wolff writes that Bach thus "may have anticipated the composition of the later [[Bach's chorale cantata cycle|series of chorale cantatas]]".{{sfn|Wolff|1991|p=157}}

It is not known when Bach composed the elaborate opening movement of the final version. Dürr suggests 1735, because in that year Bach wrote [[Wär Gott nicht mit uns diese Zeit, BWV 14|''Wär Gott nicht mit uns diese Zeit'', BWV 14]], which had an opening chorus of a comparable structure and was also based on a hymn by Luther.{{sfn|Dürr|2006|p=709}} The final version includes all stanzas of the hymn{{sfn|Dürr|2006|pp=709–710}} but is nonetheless not in the format of Bach's second cantata cycle begun in 1724; in that [[chorale cantata cycle]], each cantata relies exclusively on one Lutheran hymn.

Bach's son [[Wilhelm Friedemann Bach]], who inherited the scores, later adapted the first and fifth movements, adding parts for three trumpets and timpani.{{sfn|Dürr|2006|p=707}}

== Music ==
=== Structure and scoring ===
In the later Leipzig version (80.3), Bach structured the cantata in eight movements. He scored it for four vocal soloists (soprano (S), [[alto]] (A), [[tenor]] (T) and [[Bass (voice type)|bass]] (B)) joined by a [[SATB|four-part choir]] (SATB) alongside a [[Baroque instruments|Baroque chamber ensemble]] of three [[oboe]]s (Ob), two [[oboe d'amore|oboes d'amore]] (Oa), [[oboe da caccia|oboes da caccia]] or [[Taille (instrument)|taille]] (Ta), two [[violin]]s (Vl), [[viola]] (Va), [[cello]] (Vc), and various instruments playing the [[basso continuo]] line.{{sfn|Bischof|2010}}{{sfn|Bach Digital 1|2016}} The duration is given as 30 minutes by Dürr.{{sfn|Dürr|2006|p=707}}

In the following table of the movements, the first column shows the movement number in BWV 80, with the corresponding number in BWV 80a shown in brackets. The scoring and [[Key (music)|keys]] are given for the late Leipzig version (80.3). The keys and [[time signature]]s are taken from Dürr, using the {{music|common-time|size=8px}} symbol for common time ({{music|time|4|4}}).{{sfn|Bischof|2010}}{{sfn|Dürr|2006|pp=707–709}} The instruments are shown separately for winds and strings, while the basso continuo, playing throughout, is not shown. In the first movement, there are two basso continuo lines – the first played by a cello and [[harpsichord]], and the second by a violone and organ.<ref name=":0">{{cite book|last1=Winterson|first1=Julia|title=Edecel AS/A level Anthology of Music|date=2016|publisher=Pearson Education Ltd, Peters Edition Ltd and Faber Music Ltd|location=London|isbn=9781292118369|pages=10–35}}</ref>

