=== Movements ===
[[File:Luther's Ein Feste Burg.jpg|thumb|upright=1.4|Luther's hymn]]
{{anchor|1}}

==== Chorale fantasia ====
The cantata opens with a [[chorale fantasia]] "with contrapuntal devices of awe-inspiring complexity".{{sfn|Mincham|2010}} The movement in [[D major]] and common time elaborates on the first stanza of the hymn "{{lang|de|Ein feste Burg ist unser Gott|italic=unset}}" ("A Mighty Fortress Is Our God"). It adopts a [[motet]] technique of having the instrumental and vocal lines follow each other closely. The Bach scholar [[Klaus Hofmann]] noted that the style relates to the "vocal polyphony of the sixteenth century" when the Luther hymn was written.{{sfn|Hofmann|2005}} Structurally, the movement repeats the first two phrases, adds four new shorter phrases, then concludes with another iteration of the second phrase, all performed on oboe. All four voices "discuss each phrase imitatively as a prelude to its instrumental entry" using [[fugue (music)|fugal]] devices.{{sfn|Mincham|2010}} The chorale also makes use of the cantus firmus, which is exclusively played in the basso continuo and oboe lines (in the movement's original form).<ref name=":0" />

{{anchor|2}}

==== Aria e chorale ====
[[File:Ein Feste Burg 2.png|thumb|upright=1.5|Beginning of the obbligato oboe and soprano parts during the hymn's second stanza]]
The second movement combines an [[aria]] and an embellished version of the chorale melody.{{sfn|Jones|2007|p=275}} The bass sings the free poetry in the first [[stanza]] of the hymn, "{{langr|de|Alles, was von Gott geboren}}" (Everything that is born of God),{{sfn|Dellal|2012}} while the oboe and soprano perform the second stanza, "{{langr|de|Mit unser Macht ist nichts getan}}" (Nothing can be done through our strength).{{sfn|Dellal|2012}} Like the first movement, the duet is in D major and common time.{{sfn|Traupman-Carr|2013}} The musicologist [[Richard D. P. Jones]] interpreted the theme of the [[ritornello]], played in [[unison]] by the strings, as a motto of victory corresponding to the two mentions of victory in the text.{{sfn|Jones|2007|p=275}} In the Weimar version, the instrumental quotation of the tune, used as the closing chorale, provided a structural unity to the cantata. Jones compared the "extremely florid"{{sfn|Jones|2007|p=275}} rendition of the tune, given to the soprano in both Leipzig versions (80.2 and 80.3), to the similar approach in the chorale played by the oboe d'amore in [[Erschallet, ihr Lieder, erklinget, ihr Saiten! BWV 172#5|movement 5]] of the Weimar cantata for [[Pentecost]], {{lang|de|[[Erschallet, ihr Lieder, erklinget, ihr Saiten! BWV 172|''Erschallet, ihr Lieder'', BWV 172]]|italic=unset}}.{{sfn|Jones|2007|p=275}}

{{anchor|3}}

==== Bass recitative ====
The bass next sings "{{langr|de|Erwäge doch, Kind Gottes}}" (Only consider, child of God){{sfn|Dellal|2012}} as a [[Glossary of musical terminology#secco|secco]] recitative ending in an [[arioso]], the typical style of recitatives during the Weimar period.{{sfn|Jones|2007|p=275}} It adopts [[Canon (music)|canonic imitation]] between the voice and continuo parts.{{sfn|Smith|2013}} The interaction illustrates the unity of Christians with Jesus reflected in the fragment: "dass Christi Geist mit dir sich fest verbinde" (that the spirit of Christ may be firmly united with you).{{sfn|Jones|2007|p=275}} The mystical element of this unity, which is also exemplified in the subsequent aria and the later duet, contrasts with the "combative" character of the outer movements, where the hymn tune prevails.{{sfn|Jones|2007|p=275}}

{{anchor|4}}

==== Soprano aria ====
The fourth movement, "{{langr|de|Komm in mein Herzenshaus}}" (Come into my heart's house){{sfn|Dellal|2012}} is a soprano aria with a continuo ritornello. It is characterized by extensive [[melisma]]s and a "floating and ethereal" melody.{{sfn|Mincham|2010}}{{sfn|Smith|2013}} The soprano also sings the continuo melody.{{sfn|Jones|2007|p=275}}

{{anchor|5}}

==== Central chorale ====
The central chorale presents the third stanza of the hymn, "{{langr|de|Und wenn die Welt voll Teufel wär}}" (And if the world were full of devils).{{sfn|Hofmann|2005}} The chorale melody is sung in [[unison]], an unusual practice for Bach. The melody is unadorned and performed in {{music|time|6|8}} time signature. The orchestral accompaniment becomes more agitated and complex as the movement progresses.{{sfn|Mincham|2010}}

{{anchor|6}}

==== Tenor recitative ====
The tenor recitative, "{{langr|de|So stehe dann bei Christi blutgefärbter Fahne}}" (Then stand with Christ's bloodstained flag){{sfn|Dellal|2012}} is secco; like the earlier bass recitative, it concludes with an arioso.{{sfn|Mincham|2010}} The movement includes "occasional furious melismas", used to underscore the sense of joy conveyed by the words.{{sfn|Traupman-Carr|2013}}

{{anchor|7}}

==== Aria duetto ====
An alto and tenor duet, "{{langr|de|Wie selig sind doch die, die Gott im Munde tragen}}" (How happy are they, who bear God in their mouths){{sfn|Dellal|2012}} is accompanied by continuo and [[obbligato]] violin with oboe da caccia. The movement is "submissive" in character with a texture that becomes more complex as the duet progresses, at one point including five simultaneous melodic lines. Bach uses a [[juxtaposition]] of "flowing, largely semi-quaver" instrumental parts with the vocal "crotchet/quaver rhythms" to depict the shield of the faithful; the two parts then coalesce to tell of the smiting of enemies.{{sfn|Mincham|2010}}

{{anchor|8}}

==== Closing chorale ====
The final movement is a four-part setting of the last stanza of the hymn, "{{langr|de|Das Wort sie sollen lassen stahn}}" (That word they must allow to stand).{{sfn|Mincham|2010}}{{sfn|Ambrose}} Also in D major, each of the [[SATB]] lines is doubled by one or more instruments, and each part has a smaller vocal range than in other movements to make them simpler for the congregation to sing. Each phrase ends with a pause to break up the structure, although these are not always performed.<ref name=":0" />
<score sound raw>
\header { tagline = ##f
% title = "Das Wort sie sollen lassen stahn"
% subtitle = \markup { "Closing chorale from" \italic "Ein feste Burg ist unser Gott" ", BWV 80" }
}
\layout { indent = 0 \set Score.tempoHideNote = ##t
  \context { \Voice \remove "Dynamic_engraver" }
  \context { \Score \remove "Bar_number_engraver" }
}

global = { \key d \major \partial 4 \time 4/4 \set Timing.beamExceptions = #'() }
tn = \tempo 4 = 93
tf = \tempo 4 = 30
ta = \tempo 4 = 45

soprano = \relative c'' { \global \set Staff.midiPanPosition = -0.5 \set midiInstrument = "oboe"
  \repeat volta 2 { d4\f | d d a8 (b) cis4 | d8 (cis) b4 \tf a\fermata \tn
    d | cis b a b8 (a) | g (fis e4) \tf d\fermata \tn }
  d | a' b a gis | \ta a2\fermata \tn d,4 a' | b cis \tf d\fermata \tn
  cis | d cis b2 \tf a4\fermata \tn b b a | b8 (a) g4 \tf fis\fermata \tn
  d' | cis b a b8 (a) | g (fis e4) \tf d\fermata \bar "|."
}

alto = \relative c'' { \global \set Staff.midiPanPosition = 0.5 \set midiInstrument = "flute"
  \repeat volta 2 { a4 | a8 g fis e d4 e | a,8 (a'4) gis8 e4
    b' | e,8 fis g!4 fis8 e d cis | b4 (cis) a }
  d | cis8 fis e4 e8 fis e4 | e2 d4 d8 c | b d g4 fis
  fis | fis e8 a fis4 (e8 d) | cis4 e d8 e fis4 | fis fis8 e dis4
  fis | e d!8 g g fis16 e fis4 | g8 (b,) cis4 a \bar "|."
}

tenor = \relative c { \global \set Staff.midiPanPosition = -1 \set midiInstrument = "english horn"
  \repeat volta 2 { fis'8\pp e | d e fis g a4 g | fis b,8 e cis4
    fis,8 gis | a4 e'8 d cis4 b8 cis | d4 (a8 g!) fis4 }
  fis8 g | a d cis b cis4 b | cis2 fis,8 g a4 | d e a,
  a | a8 gis a4 a (gis) | e g!8 a b cis d c | b4 b b
  b8 a | g a b4 e8 cis! d4 | d (a8 g) fis4 \bar "|."
}

bass = \relative c { \global \set Staff.midiPanPosition = 1 \set midiInstrument = "bassoon"
  \repeat volta 2 { d'8 cis | b4 a8 g fis g fis e | d4 e a,
    b | cis8 d e4 fis g8 a | b [g a a,] d4 }
  d8 e | fis4 gis a8 d, e4 | a,2 b4 fis' | g! e d
  fis | b, cis d ( e) | a, e'8 fis g4 d | dis e b
  b | e8 fis g e cis a d! cis | b (g a4) d,4 \bar "|."
}

verse = \new Lyrics \lyricmode {
  << { Das Wort sie sol -- len las -- sen stahn
    und kein' Dank da -- zu ha -- ben; }
    \new Lyrics \lyricmode {
      er ist bei uns wohl auf dem Plan
      mit sei -- nem Geist und Ga -- ben. }
  >>
  Neh -- men sie uns den Leib,
  Gut, Ehr, Kind und Weib:
  lass fah -- ren da -- hin,
  sie ha -- ben's kein' Ge -- winn,
  das Reich muss uns doch blei -- ben.
}

\score {
  \new ChoirStaff <<
    \new Staff
    <<
      \new Voice = "soprano" { \voiceOne \soprano }
      \new Voice = "alto" { \voiceTwo \alto }
    >>
    \new Lyrics
      \lyricsto "soprano" \verse
    \new Staff
    <<
      \clef bass
      \new Voice = "tenor" { \voiceOne \tenor }
      \new Voice = "bass" { \voiceTwo \bass }
    >>
  >>
  \layout { }
}
\score { \unfoldRepeats { << \soprano \\ \alto \\ \tenor \\ \bass >> }
  \midi { \tempo 4=93
    \context { \Score midiChannelMapping = #'instrument }
    \context { \Staff \remove "Staff_performer" }
    \context { \Voice \consists "Staff_performer" }
  }
}
</score>

== Reception ==
=== Manuscripts and publication ===
The oldest extant manuscript of {{langr|de|Ein feste Burg ist unser Gott}} is by Bach's student and son-in-law [[Johann Christoph Altnikol]].{{sfn|Wolff|1991|p=158}} The cantata was published in 1821,{{sfn|Bach Digital 4|2017}} the first [[Bach cantata]] to be published after the composer's death.{{sfn|Wolff|1991|p=152}} The [[Bach Gesellschaft]] edition of the cantata, published half a century later, was printed with Wilhelm Friedemann Bach's extended instrumentation. This version was edited by [[Wilhelm Rust]] and appeared in volume 18, published in 1870 by [[Breitkopf & Härtel]].{{sfn|Wolff|1991|pp=157–158}} The [[New Bach Edition]] (Neue Bach-Ausgabe, NBA) published the score of the Leipzig version (80.3) in 1987, edited by [[Frieder Rempp]], with critical commentary following in 1988.{{sfn|Bach Digital 1|2016}}

=== Arrangements ===
[[Theodore Thomas (conductor)|Theodore Thomas]] arranged the cantata for modern orchestra. This arrangement was performed in New York in the late 1800s, and was praised for "religiously preserving the spirit of the original".<ref>Hassard, cited in {{cite book|editor=Stinson, Russell|title=Bach perspectives|date=2003|publisher=University of Nebraska Press|isbn=9780252027888|pages=[https://archive.org/details/bachinamerica0000unse/page/75 75&ndash;76]|url=https://archive.org/details/bachinamerica0000unse/page/75}}</ref>

=== Evaluation ===
Upon its initial publication in 1821, the cantata was positively received, as demonstrated by a laudatory 1822 article by critic [[Johann Friedrich Rochlitz]], which praised its "profound, highly original and&mdash;one might say&mdash;unimaginable wealth of sound".{{sfn|Wolff|1991|p=152}}<ref>Cited in Johann Forkel and Charles Terry (1920). ''Johann Sebastian Bach: his life, work, and art''. Harcourt, Brace, and Howe. p. xvii.</ref> Christoph Wolff suggests that this cantata "acquired not only the character of prototype for Bach's church cantatas but also that of paragon of Protestant chorale composition" as the "musical symbol of Lutheranism".{{sfn|Wolff|1991|p=152}} [[Giacomo Meyerbeer]] cited this edition as exemplifying Lutheran music.<ref>{{cite book|editor1=Portebois, Yannick|editor2=Terpstra, Nicholas|title=The Renaissance in the nineteenth century : Le XIXe siècle renaissant|date=2003|publisher=Centre for Reformation and Renaissance Studies, Victoria University|isbn=9780772720191|page=[https://archive.org/details/renaissanceinnin0000unse/page/134 134]|url-access=registration|url=https://archive.org/details/renaissanceinnin0000unse/page/134}}</ref> The 1870 edition, in contrast, was incorporated into the growing German nationalist movement, to the point that it was a standard for German military broadcasts during the [[Second World War]].{{sfn|Wolff|1991|p=154}}

Modern musicologists agree that the chorale fantasia is an outstanding composition. For example, [[Craig Smith (conductor)|Craig Smith]] suggested that "in a genre in which Bach was the absolute master, this is probably the greatest motet chorus".{{sfn|Smith|2013}} Wolff wrote: "An immense chorale motet of 228 measures, it is one of Bach's most elaborate choral compositions and of the most impressive high points in the history of the chorale cantata."{{sfn|Wolff|1991|pp=157–158}}

=== Performances ===
Details of early performances of the work's versions are unknown. The 1982 Zwang catalogue places the first performance of BWV&nbsp;80's early chorale cantata version in 1724.<ref>Philippe (and Gérard) Zwang. ''Guide pratique des cantates de Bach''. Paris, 1982. {{ISBN|2-221-00749-2}}</ref> The first and fifth movements with Wilhelm Friedemann's extended instrumentation were performed with a new Latin text in 1763 in celebration of the end of the [[Seven Years' War]].{{sfn|Schulenberg|2010|p=[https://books.google.com/books?id=MTwEjTuofJwC&pg=PA237 237]}}

The first American performance may have occurred as early as 1823 in [[Bethlehem, Pennsylvania]]; handmade copies of the parts dating to that time are held by the [[Moravian Archives]].<ref>{{cite book|last1=Larson|first1=Paul S.|title=Bach for one hundred years : a social history of the Bach Choir of Bethlehem|date=2012|publisher=Lehigh University Press|isbn=9781611460940}}</ref> Other sources suggest an initial performance date of 1865 at [[Harvard University]].<ref>{{cite book|editor=Stinson, Russell|title=Bach perspectives|date=2003|publisher=University of Nebraska Press|isbn=9780252027888|page=[https://archive.org/details/bachinamerica0000unse/page/3 3]|url=https://archive.org/details/bachinamerica0000unse/page/3}}</ref>

